# Issues and the board: gh dojo

This project tracks the issues of acme/payments on the GitHub Projects board of acme, project 12, with the process in `.github/dojo.toml`. Take, move, comment on, block and create issues **only** through `gh dojo`: never change a status, a comment, a blocked-by link or an issue with `gh issue`, `gh project` or the API. For what gh dojo cannot do, such as editing an issue's title, body, labels or assignees, use `gh issue` only when the task needs it or the user asks.

Everything below is enough for everyday work; read `gh dojo <command> --help` only when a command refuses or you need a flag not named here. When the setup is broken (token, board, config), `gh dojo doctor` names each problem with the command that fixes it.

A bare `12` or `#12` is an issue of `acme/payments`; an issue of another repository is `owner/repo#12` or its URL.

## Lifecycle

`Backlog` → `In progress` → `In review` → `Approved` → `Merged`, through `start`, `review`, `approve`, `merged`. Each verb below says which statuses it moves from and to.

- `Backlog` is the initial status: `gh dojo new` puts new issues there, and an issue without a status or not on the board counts as `Backlog`.
- Final: `Merged`. `gh dojo list` hides issues there unless `--all`.
- A blocker is resolved, no longer blocking, when it is closed or its board status is `Merged`: its work is done though the issue may be open.
- A blocker whose status could not be read (`status not read: …`) counts as unresolved.

## Verbs

Run a verb as `gh dojo <verb> N` (`N`, `#N` or `owner/repo#N`); `--dry-run` prints its plan (status, links, the comment it would post) without changing anything. A verb adds the issue to the board if it is missing and refuses a closed issue unless noted. It confirms in one line, such as `#42 Backlog → In progress, comment left`: there is no need to read the issue again.

A verb that takes several numbers at once checks and writes each issue on its own, skips one that fails and names each issue not done in a summary; the same command again touches only what is left.

### `start`

**When:** You begin writing the change

`gh dojo start N`: Backlog → In progress; optional --comment; assigns you; comments "Started. Session: {session} {comment}".

- It is refused (exit code 2) while a blocker is unresolved: `gh dojo show N` lists them. Never move the status another way.

### `hold`

**When:** Work is stuck: waiting for another issue or an outside decision

`gh dojo hold N`: In progress, Changes requested → status unchanged; needs --by or --comment; labels +blocked; comments "On hold until {by}. {comment}".

- `--by 12` (also `#12` or an issue's URL; several comma-separated, `--by 12,13`) also marks the issue blocked by #12. Text (`--by "the user's decision"`) only goes into the comment; issues mixed with text are an error (exit code 1); a pull request is named in the comment without a link.

### `resume`

**When:** What held the issue is resolved

`gh dojo resume N`: In progress, Changes requested, Approved → status unchanged; optional --comment; labels -blocked; comments "{comment}".

- It is refused (exit code 2) while a blocker is unresolved: `gh dojo show N` lists them. Never move the status another way.
- It comments only when given `--comment`.

### `review`

**When:** The pull request is open and ready for review

`gh dojo review N`: In progress, Changes requested → In review; needs --comment and --assignee; refused with the label blocked; labels +needs-review -changes-requested; assigns the --assignee user; comments "Ready for review by @{assignee}: {comment}".

- It is refused (exit code 2) while the issue has the label `blocked`. `resume` removes `blocked`. Never remove a label another way to get it through; ask the user.
- It is refused (exit code 2) while a blocker is unresolved: `gh dojo show N` lists them. Never move the status another way.
- `--assignee <login>` names the user to assign (`--assignee @me` is you); one who cannot be assigned issues in the repository is exit code 1, with the list of who can. Ask the user whom to assign if you do not know.

### `request-changes`

**When:** The reviewer asked for changes; the author makes them and runs review again

`gh dojo request-changes N`: In review → Changes requested; needs --comment; labels +changes-requested -needs-review; assigns you; unassigns everyone else; comments "Changes requested: {comment}".

### `approve`

**When:** The user approved the pull request after reviewing it

`gh dojo approve N`: In review → Approved; optional --comment; refused with the label blocked; labels -needs-review; assigns you; unassigns everyone else; comments "Approved. {comment}".

- It is the user's decision: without `--yes` it exits with code 2. Ask the user, and run it again with `--yes` only if they agree; never add `--yes` on your own.
- It is refused (exit code 2) while the issue has the label `blocked`. `resume` removes `blocked`. Never remove a label another way to get it through; ask the user.

### `reject`

**When:** The user decided the change is not wanted

`gh dojo reject N`: In progress, In review, Changes requested, Approved → status unchanged; needs --comment; on a closed issue only if closed as not planned; labels -needs-review -changes-requested -blocked; comments "{comment}"; closes as not planned.

- It is the user's decision: without `--yes` it exits with code 2. Ask the user, and run it again with `--yes` only if they agree; never add `--yes` on your own.
- An issue closed already stays closed as it is.

### `merged`

**When:** The approved pull request has been merged into main

`gh dojo merged N`: Approved → Merged; optional --comment; also on closed issues; refused with the label blocked; comments "{comment}"; closes as completed.

- It is refused (exit code 2) while the issue has the label `blocked`. `resume` removes `blocked`. Never remove a label another way to get it through; ask the user.
- It takes several numbers at once: `gh dojo merged 12 13 14`.
- For a release, `gh dojo release <tag> --verb merged --dry-run` lists the issues the release includes and its plan; read `gh dojo release --help` before you run it.
- It comments only when given `--comment`.
- An issue closed already stays closed as it is.

## Reading

- `gh dojo next`: what to take next, the open issues in `Backlog` or without a status, with no unresolved blocker, in board order, 5 by default (`--limit N`). The blocked ones are hidden and counted; `--all` shows them with their blockers. Filters: `--mine`, `--label <label>`. Once you pick one, `gh dojo show N`, then `gh dojo start N`.
- `gh dojo show N` before you work on an issue: its status, milestone, labels, blockers both ways (unresolved first, each with its board status), its parent and sub-issues (the same way), status history, description and the last 5 comments; a line left out means none. Several numbers, `gh dojo show 12 13`, print three lines per issue: read the one you take alone. `--comments all` prints the whole thread (up to 100), `--full` everything uncut.
- `gh dojo list`: an overview of the board, when the user asks for one: the open issues grouped by status; closed issues and final statuses are hidden and counted. Filters: `--status "<status>"`, `--status none`, `--mine`, `--label <label>`, `--all`, `--limit 100`.

## Creating and linking issues

- `gh dojo new "<title>" --type <bug|feature|chore> --body "…"`: the issue, its labels, the board in `Backlog`, in one command. A long body: `--body-file <file>`, or `--body-file -` for stdin. More labels: `--label X`. `--area X` adds the label `service:X`. `--milestone "<title>"`: an open milestone, by its title or a start only that title has (none or several is exit code 1 with the list). `--blocked-by 12`: blocked from the start (repeat it, or comma-separate; an issue that does not exist is exit code 3, and nothing is created). `--parent 12`: a sub-issue of #12 (one issue; one that does not exist is exit code 3, and nothing is created). A title starting with `-` goes after `--`, and every flag before it: `gh dojo new --type bug -- "-v is ignored"`.
- `gh dojo comment N "text"`, or `gh dojo comment N --body-file -`: a comment without changing the status.
- `gh dojo block N --by M` marks N blocked by M without changing the status; `gh dojo unblock N --by M` removes that. `--by` repeats or takes a comma-separated list. A link already there (or already gone) is not an error: the line says `already`. A pull request cannot block (exit code 3), nor can an issue N blocks (a cycle, exit code 1).
- An existing issue becomes a sub-issue with `gh issue edit N --parent M` (`--remove-parent` undoes it): gh-dojo has no command for it, since the link changes no status.

## Repeats are safe

Run the same command again after an error: a verb does not set a status that is already set, nor post a comment that is already the latest from gh-dojo; `comment` does not post its last comment again; `block` and `unblock` skip links that are already as asked; `new` with the same title finishes the issue it created instead of creating another.

## Session

`{session}` in a comment is a link to your agent session, from `CLAUDE_CODE_HOST_SESSION_ID` (the first one set). When none is set the verb warns and still runs.

## Exit codes

On an error, read its `error:`, `allowed:` and `fix:` lines and do what `fix:` says. Exit code 2 is a process check refusing: never work around it (do not move the status another way); ask the user. On 4 (network, rate limit or auth) and 5 (partially applied), run the same command again, on 4 a bit later.

## JSON

Every command takes `--json`, `--jq '<expression>'` and `--template`, as gh does; an error is then JSON too (`error` with `exit_code`, `message`, `allowed`, `fix`), with the same exit code. Use it to pick values out of the output; for everyday work the text is shorter.

```sh
gh dojo list --jq '.issues[] | select(.status == "Merged") | .number'
gh dojo show 42 --jq '.issues[0].blocked_by[] | select(.resolved | not) | .ref'
```
